1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,310
Emma, put the phone down.

2
00:00:02,490 --> 00:00:03,953
Jack, you too.

3
00:00:04,133 --> 00:00:06,037
Come here, both of you.

4
00:00:06,217 --> 00:00:08,311
You've been asking me for years to

5
00:00:08,311 --> 00:00:09,851
tell you the whole story.

6
00:00:10,031 --> 00:00:11,761
Not the nice parts.

7
00:00:11,941 --> 00:00:13,148
The whole thing.

8
00:00:13,328 --> 00:00:14,629
Where I come from,

9
00:00:14,629 --> 00:00:17,883
and why your great-grandmother never spoke to

10
00:00:17,883 --> 00:00:19,690
her brother-in-law again.

11
00:00:19,870 --> 00:00:22,123
Well, I'm eighty-two now.

12
00:00:22,303 --> 00:00:24,263
And if I don't tell it,

13
00:00:24,263 --> 00:00:25,286
nobody will.

14
00:00:25,466 --> 00:00:27,231
So we're going to start at the

15
00:00:27,231 --> 00:00:28,113
very beginning.

16
00:00:28,293 --> 00:00:29,930
The week I was born.

17
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:32,702
It will take me a long time,

18
00:00:32,702 --> 00:00:33,740
sweethearts.

19
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:35,946
A whole year of nights,

20
00:00:35,946 --> 00:00:36,474
maybe.

21
00:00:36,654 --> 00:00:38,685
There are things in this story I

22
00:00:38,685 --> 00:00:40,463
have never told your mother.

23
00:00:40,643 --> 00:00:42,970
There are things I didn't know myself

24
00:00:42,970 --> 00:00:44,858
until I was a grown woman with

25
00:00:44,858 --> 00:00:46,053
children of my own.

26
00:00:46,233 --> 00:00:48,044
But I promise you this.

27
00:00:48,224 --> 00:00:51,254
Every night, I'll tell you one piece.

28
00:00:51,434 --> 00:00:53,466
And every piece is true.

29
00:00:53,816 --> 00:00:56,999
It was August of nineteen forty-four,

30
00:00:56,999 --> 00:00:58,634
in Mansfield, Ohio.

31
00:00:58,814 --> 00:01:00,974
The war had been going on for

32
00:01:00,974 --> 00:01:02,390
almost three years.

33
00:01:02,570 --> 00:01:04,888
Every house on Maple Street had a

34
00:01:04,888 --> 00:01:07,136
little flag in the front window,

35
00:01:07,136 --> 00:01:09,173
white with a red border and a

36
00:01:09,173 --> 00:01:10,859
blue star in the middle.

37
00:01:11,039 --> 00:01:13,805
A blue star meant somebody from that

38
00:01:13,805 --> 00:01:15,265
house was fighting.

39
00:01:15,445 --> 00:01:18,084
A gold star meant they weren't coming

40
00:01:18,084 --> 00:01:18,440
home.

41
00:01:18,791 --> 00:01:21,380
Our house had one blue star.

42
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:23,308
It was for my father,

43
00:01:23,308 --> 00:01:24,474
Walter Miller.

44
00:01:24,654 --> 00:01:26,792
He was twenty-six years old,

45
00:01:26,792 --> 00:01:28,778
a private in the infantry,

46
00:01:28,778 --> 00:01:31,375
and he had shipped out the October

47
00:01:31,375 --> 00:01:31,910
before.

48
00:01:32,090 --> 00:01:35,492
Nobody knew exactly where he was.

49
00:01:35,672 --> 00:01:38,518
The letters came with black lines through

50
00:01:38,518 --> 00:01:39,767
half of the words,

51
00:01:39,767 --> 00:01:42,336
so the enemy wouldn't learn anything.

52
00:01:42,516 --> 00:01:44,857
My mother read them anyway,

53
00:01:44,857 --> 00:01:46,071
over and over,

54
00:01:46,071 --> 00:01:48,239
until the paper was soft.

55
00:01:48,590 --> 00:01:50,586
My mother was Dorothy.

56
00:01:50,766 --> 00:01:52,915
She was twenty-three that summer,

57
00:01:52,915 --> 00:01:55,063
and she was the prettiest girl on

58
00:01:55,063 --> 00:01:55,910
Maple Street.

59
00:01:56,090 --> 00:01:58,075
Everybody said so.

60
00:01:58,255 --> 00:02:00,425
She had dark hair she pinned up

61
00:02:00,425 --> 00:02:02,595
in rolls, and she sang when she

62
00:02:02,595 --> 00:02:03,085
cooked.

63
00:02:03,265 --> 00:02:05,331
She had wanted to be a teacher.

64
00:02:05,511 --> 00:02:06,917
Then the war came,

65
00:02:06,917 --> 00:02:09,494
and Walter asked her to marry him

66
00:02:09,494 --> 00:02:11,602
three weeks before he left,

67
00:02:11,602 --> 00:02:12,930
and she said yes.

68
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:16,412
She lived with Grandma Pearl while Walter

69
00:02:16,412 --> 00:02:17,099
was gone.

70
00:02:17,279 --> 00:02:19,048
That was Walter's mother,

71
00:02:19,048 --> 00:02:21,099
born in eighteen ninety-five.

72
00:02:21,279 --> 00:02:23,489
A tough old bird who had buried

73
00:02:23,489 --> 00:02:26,126
a husband and raised two boys through

74
00:02:26,126 --> 00:02:28,907
the Depression on a seamstress's wages.

75
00:02:29,087 --> 00:02:31,118
Pearl didn't trust banks,

76
00:02:31,118 --> 00:02:32,825
didn't trust doctors,

77
00:02:32,825 --> 00:02:35,995
and didn't trust anybody who smiled too

78
00:02:35,995 --> 00:02:36,401
much.

79
00:02:36,581 --> 00:02:39,019
That last part will matter later.

80
00:02:39,369 --> 00:02:41,632
They were married at the courthouse on

81
00:02:41,632 --> 00:02:44,430
a Friday afternoon in September of forty-three.

82
00:02:44,610 --> 00:02:47,559
No church, no white dress.

83
00:02:47,739 --> 00:02:49,044
There wasn't time,

84
00:02:49,044 --> 00:02:50,712
and there wasn't money.

85
00:02:50,892 --> 00:02:53,489
Mother wore a blue suit she borrowed

86
00:02:53,489 --> 00:02:54,644
from her sister,

87
00:02:54,644 --> 00:02:57,530
and she carried three roses from Grandma

88
00:02:57,530 --> 00:02:58,612
Pearl's garden.

89
00:02:58,792 --> 00:03:01,799
There's one photograph of that day.

90
00:03:01,979 --> 00:03:04,213
Walter is in his uniform,

91
00:03:04,213 --> 00:03:04,928
smiling.

92
00:03:05,108 --> 00:03:07,666
And standing right beside him,

93
00:03:07,666 --> 00:03:09,030
as his best man,

94
00:03:09,030 --> 00:03:10,820
is his brother Frank.

95
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:12,893
Remember that photograph.

96
00:03:13,073 --> 00:03:14,698
We'll come back to it.

97
00:03:15,048 --> 00:03:17,678
Walter had three weeks of leave before

98
00:03:17,678 --> 00:03:18,716
he shipped out.

99
00:03:18,896 --> 00:03:21,240
They spent them in the little back

100
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:22,963
bedroom at Pearl's house,

101
00:03:22,963 --> 00:03:25,514
because there was nowhere else to go.

102
00:03:25,694 --> 00:03:27,872
Then on the second of October,

103
00:03:27,872 --> 00:03:30,413
Mother and Pearl and Frank took him

104
00:03:30,413 --> 00:03:32,590
to the train station downtown.

105
00:03:32,770 --> 00:03:35,365
The platform was full of boys in

106
00:03:35,365 --> 00:03:37,554
uniform and girls in tears.

107
00:03:37,734 --> 00:03:39,963
Walter kissed my mother goodbye,

108
00:03:39,963 --> 00:03:41,844
and then he put his hand on

109
00:03:41,844 --> 00:03:43,655
Frank's shoulder and said,

110
00:03:43,655 --> 00:03:45,327
take care of her for me.

111
00:03:45,507 --> 00:03:47,132
Frank said he would.

112
00:03:47,482 --> 00:03:50,095
You have to understand what that summer

113
00:03:50,095 --> 00:03:50,698
was like.

114
00:03:50,878 --> 00:03:52,584
Everything was rationed.

115
00:03:52,764 --> 00:03:55,469
Sugar, coffee, butter,

116
00:03:55,469 --> 00:03:58,174
meat, gasoline, shoes.

117
00:03:58,354 --> 00:04:00,593
You got a little book of stamps,

118
00:04:00,593 --> 00:04:02,692
and when the stamps were gone,

119
00:04:02,692 --> 00:04:03,881
you went without.

120
00:04:04,061 --> 00:04:06,426
Mother saved bacon grease in a can

121
00:04:06,426 --> 00:04:08,304
by the stove and took it to

122
00:04:08,304 --> 00:04:10,947
the butcher, because they used the fat

123
00:04:10,947 --> 00:04:12,269
to make explosives.

124
00:04:12,449 --> 00:04:14,678
Every woman on the street did.

125
00:04:15,029 --> 00:04:17,959
The factories in Mansfield ran day and

126
00:04:17,959 --> 00:04:19,347
night for the war.

127
00:04:19,527 --> 00:04:21,647
The Westinghouse plant,

128
00:04:21,647 --> 00:04:23,030
the steel mill,

129
00:04:23,030 --> 00:04:24,044
Ohio Brass.

130
00:04:24,224 --> 00:04:26,871
Women worked on the lines in coveralls

131
00:04:26,871 --> 00:04:29,309
with their hair tied up in scarves.

132
00:04:29,489 --> 00:04:30,989
My mother couldn't,

133
00:04:30,989 --> 00:04:32,252
not that summer.

134
00:04:32,432 --> 00:04:34,891
She was eight months along with me,

135
00:04:34,891 --> 00:04:35,944
big as a house,

136
00:04:35,944 --> 00:04:37,982
she said, and hot as a stove.

137
00:04:38,332 --> 00:04:40,339
And there was one man on Maple

138
00:04:40,339 --> 00:04:42,279
Street who wasn't at the war.

139
00:04:42,459 --> 00:04:44,607
My father's younger brother.

140
00:04:44,787 --> 00:04:45,495
Frank.

141
00:04:45,675 --> 00:04:47,657
Uncle Frank was twenty-four,

142
00:04:47,657 --> 00:04:48,930
tall and handsome,

143
00:04:48,930 --> 00:04:51,195
with a laugh you could hear from

144
00:04:51,195 --> 00:04:52,327
two houses down.

145
00:04:52,507 --> 00:04:54,747
The Army had turned him away because

146
00:04:54,747 --> 00:04:56,490
of something with his heart.

147
00:04:56,670 --> 00:04:58,748
He worked at the steel mill and

148
00:04:58,748 --> 00:05:01,295
came by our house almost every evening

149
00:05:01,295 --> 00:05:02,635
to check on Dorothy,

150
00:05:02,635 --> 00:05:03,171
he said.

151
00:05:03,351 --> 00:05:05,615
To see if she needed anything.

152
00:05:05,966 --> 00:05:08,813
Frank and Walter had always been like

153
00:05:08,813 --> 00:05:10,122
that, Pearl said.

154
00:05:10,302 --> 00:05:12,094
Walter was the older one,

155
00:05:12,094 --> 00:05:13,240
the serious one,

156
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:15,462
the one who got the good grades

157
00:05:15,462 --> 00:05:17,827
and the steady job and the pretty

158
00:05:17,827 --> 00:05:18,185
girl.

159
00:05:18,365 --> 00:05:20,188
Frank was the fun one.

160
00:05:20,368 --> 00:05:22,061
And Frank had always,

161
00:05:22,061 --> 00:05:24,803
always wanted whatever Walter had.

162
00:05:24,983 --> 00:05:26,439
When they were boys,

163
00:05:26,439 --> 00:05:28,405
if Walter got a new pair of

164
00:05:28,405 --> 00:05:30,953
shoes, Frank would cry until he got

165
00:05:30,953 --> 00:05:31,972
the same ones.

166
00:05:32,152 --> 00:05:34,416
Pearl thought he'd grown out of it.

167
00:05:34,596 --> 00:05:35,606
She was wrong.

168
00:05:35,956 --> 00:05:38,833
Grandma Pearl watched him every time he

169
00:05:38,833 --> 00:05:40,530
came through that door.

170
00:05:40,710 --> 00:05:42,300
She never said anything.

171
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:44,001
She just watched.

172
00:05:44,181 --> 00:05:46,691
I didn't understand that look until I

173
00:05:46,691 --> 00:05:48,047
was forty years old.

174
00:05:48,227 --> 00:05:50,271
But I'm getting ahead of myself.

175
00:05:50,621 --> 00:05:53,267
On Monday, the seventh of August,

176
00:05:53,267 --> 00:05:56,315
the Western Union boy rode his bicycle

177
00:05:56,315 --> 00:05:57,598
up Maple Street.

178
00:05:57,778 --> 00:06:00,739
Sweethearts, you don't know what that meant.

179
00:06:00,919 --> 00:06:02,102
In those days,

180
00:06:02,102 --> 00:06:05,314
every mother and every wife in America

181
00:06:05,314 --> 00:06:07,258
was afraid of that boy.

182
00:06:07,438 --> 00:06:10,606
Because the telegrams from the War Department

183
00:06:10,606 --> 00:06:11,873
came on a bicycle.

184
00:06:12,053 --> 00:06:14,376
And every woman on the street came

185
00:06:14,376 --> 00:06:16,495
out onto her porch to see which

186
00:06:16,495 --> 00:06:18,067
house he would stop at.

187
00:06:18,417 --> 00:06:20,309
He stopped at ours.

188
00:06:20,489 --> 00:06:21,580
Mrs.

189
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:23,937
Pruitt next door told me later that

190
00:06:23,937 --> 00:06:26,176
my mother was hanging laundry in the

191
00:06:26,176 --> 00:06:28,291
back, and she came around the side

192
00:06:28,291 --> 00:06:30,530
of the house with a clothespin still

193
00:06:30,530 --> 00:06:31,339
in her mouth.

194
00:06:31,519 --> 00:06:34,003
The boy was only fifteen.

195
00:06:34,183 --> 00:06:36,295
He held out the yellow envelope,

196
00:06:36,295 --> 00:06:38,142
and he couldn't look at her.

197
00:06:38,492 --> 00:06:39,879
The telegram said,

198
00:06:39,879 --> 00:06:42,500
The Secretary of War desires me to

199
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:45,738
express his deep regret that your husband,

200
00:06:45,738 --> 00:06:47,048
Private Walter J.

201
00:06:47,228 --> 00:06:50,313
Miller, has been reported missing in action

202
00:06:50,313 --> 00:06:53,254
since the twenty-fifth of July in France.

203
00:06:53,434 --> 00:06:54,722
That was all.

204
00:06:54,902 --> 00:06:55,715
Missing.

205
00:06:55,895 --> 00:06:56,998
Not dead.

206
00:06:57,178 --> 00:06:58,304
Not alive.

207
00:06:58,484 --> 00:06:59,239
Missing.

208
00:06:59,419 --> 00:07:01,690
In those days a word like that

209
00:07:01,690 --> 00:07:03,355
could last for months.

210
00:07:03,705 --> 00:07:05,594
My mother didn't cry,

211
00:07:05,594 --> 00:07:07,304
Grandma Pearl said.

212
00:07:07,484 --> 00:07:09,716
She folded the telegram in half,

213
00:07:09,716 --> 00:07:11,879
and put it in her apron pocket,

214
00:07:11,879 --> 00:07:14,530
and went back and finished hanging the

215
00:07:14,530 --> 00:07:15,088
laundry.

216
00:07:15,268 --> 00:07:16,325
Every sheet.

217
00:07:16,505 --> 00:07:18,754
Then she went inside and sat down

218
00:07:18,754 --> 00:07:20,185
at the kitchen table.

219
00:07:20,365 --> 00:07:22,455
And that's when her water broke.

220
00:07:22,805 --> 00:07:24,360
There was no car.

221
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:26,759
Grandma Pearl didn't drive,

222
00:07:26,759 --> 00:07:29,800
and the gasoline was rationed anyway.

223
00:07:29,980 --> 00:07:32,271
So Pearl ran next door to the

224
00:07:32,271 --> 00:07:33,614
Pruitts, and Mrs.

225
00:07:33,794 --> 00:07:35,832
Pruitt ran to the corner to use

226
00:07:35,832 --> 00:07:37,870
the telephone at the drugstore,

227
00:07:37,870 --> 00:07:40,237
and the only person who answered was

228
00:07:40,237 --> 00:07:40,632
Frank.

229
00:07:40,812 --> 00:07:42,808
He came in his truck from the

230
00:07:42,808 --> 00:07:44,736
mill, still black with soot,

231
00:07:44,736 --> 00:07:46,801
and he carried my mother to it

232
00:07:46,801 --> 00:07:47,627
in his arms.

233
00:07:47,977 --> 00:07:50,692
I was born at Mansfield General Hospital

234
00:07:50,692 --> 00:07:52,864
at four in the morning on August

235
00:07:52,864 --> 00:07:55,035
the eighth, nineteen forty-four.

236
00:07:55,215 --> 00:07:57,468
Seven pounds, two ounces.

237
00:07:57,648 --> 00:07:59,610
Dark hair like my mother.

238
00:07:59,790 --> 00:08:01,980
The nurse wrote my name down as

239
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:03,110
Ruth Ann Miller.

240
00:08:03,290 --> 00:08:05,891
Ruth after nobody in particular.

241
00:08:06,071 --> 00:08:08,219
My mother just liked it.

242
00:08:08,399 --> 00:08:10,256
She said it meant a friend.

243
00:08:10,607 --> 00:08:13,626
Fathers weren't allowed in the delivery room

244
00:08:13,626 --> 00:08:15,959
back then, even if they were home.

245
00:08:16,139 --> 00:08:18,520
They waited in a little room down

246
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:19,819
the hall, smoking.

247
00:08:19,999 --> 00:08:22,428
But my father was in France,

248
00:08:22,428 --> 00:08:23,122
missing.

249
00:08:23,302 --> 00:08:25,758
So the man waiting in that little

250
00:08:25,758 --> 00:08:27,321
room, all night long,

251
00:08:27,321 --> 00:08:28,735
pacing up and down,

252
00:08:28,735 --> 00:08:29,479
was Frank.

253
00:08:29,829 --> 00:08:32,276
The nurse came out and told him,

254
00:08:32,276 --> 00:08:33,193
it's a girl,

255
00:08:33,193 --> 00:08:34,264
Mister Miller.

256
00:08:34,444 --> 00:08:36,638
And Frank didn't correct her.

257
00:08:36,818 --> 00:08:37,851
Mrs.

258
00:08:38,031 --> 00:08:39,273
Pruitt was there.

259
00:08:39,453 --> 00:08:40,313
She heard it.

260
00:08:40,493 --> 00:08:42,582
She never forgot it either.

261
00:08:42,933 --> 00:08:45,162
We came home on the fourteenth.

262
00:08:45,342 --> 00:08:47,515
In those days they kept you in

263
00:08:47,515 --> 00:08:49,254
the hospital for a week.

264
00:08:49,434 --> 00:08:52,003
Mother carried me up the porch steps,

265
00:08:52,003 --> 00:08:54,363
and Grandma Pearl had put a little

266
00:08:54,363 --> 00:08:56,376
white crib in the front room,

267
00:08:56,376 --> 00:08:58,874
right under the window with the blue

268
00:08:58,874 --> 00:08:59,221
star.

269
00:08:59,401 --> 00:09:01,319
And every day that week,

270
00:09:01,319 --> 00:09:04,115
Mother sat in the rocking chair and

271
00:09:04,115 --> 00:09:05,713
looked at that star,

272
00:09:05,713 --> 00:09:07,470
and then looked at me.

273
00:09:07,821 --> 00:09:09,230
The neighbors came,

274
00:09:09,230 --> 00:09:10,862
the way neighbors did.

275
00:09:11,042 --> 00:09:14,522
They brought casseroles and baby blankets and

276
00:09:14,522 --> 00:09:15,141
prayers.

277
00:09:15,321 --> 00:09:17,993
The ladies from the Lutheran church came

278
00:09:17,993 --> 00:09:18,862
on Wednesday.

279
00:09:19,042 --> 00:09:20,422
Each one held me,

280
00:09:20,422 --> 00:09:21,964
and said how sweet,

281
00:09:21,964 --> 00:09:24,886
and then lowered her voice and asked

282
00:09:24,886 --> 00:09:27,645
Dorothy if there was any news from

283
00:09:27,645 --> 00:09:28,214
France.

284
00:09:28,394 --> 00:09:29,311
There wasn't.

285
00:09:29,491 --> 00:09:30,687
Not that week.

286
00:09:30,867 --> 00:09:32,597
Not for a long time.

287
00:09:32,947 --> 00:09:34,885
Frank came every evening.

288
00:09:35,065 --> 00:09:37,264
He brought a rattle he had carved

289
00:09:37,264 --> 00:09:39,396
himself out of a piece of maple.

290
00:09:39,576 --> 00:09:40,967
He brought oranges,

291
00:09:40,967 --> 00:09:42,943
which you could hardly get.

292
00:09:43,123 --> 00:09:44,962
He sat on the porch with my

293
00:09:44,962 --> 00:09:46,188
mother until dark.

294
00:09:46,368 --> 00:09:48,026
They talked quietly,

295
00:09:48,026 --> 00:09:49,932
so Pearl couldn't hear.

296
00:09:50,112 --> 00:09:52,381
And Pearl sat in the kitchen with

297
00:09:52,381 --> 00:09:53,344
the light off,

298
00:09:53,344 --> 00:09:54,512
listening anyway.

299
00:09:54,863 --> 00:09:57,785
On Saturday, the nineteenth of August,

300
00:09:57,785 --> 00:10:00,170
the news came on the radio that

301
00:10:00,170 --> 00:10:02,247
Paris was about to be free.

302
00:10:02,427 --> 00:10:05,243
Everybody on Maple Street came out into

303
00:10:05,243 --> 00:10:06,037
the street.

304
00:10:06,217 --> 00:10:07,529
People hugged.

305
00:10:07,709 --> 00:10:08,522
Mr.

306
00:10:08,702 --> 00:10:10,385
Pruitt rang the dinner bell.

307
00:10:10,565 --> 00:10:12,841
And my mother stood on the porch

308
00:10:12,841 --> 00:10:15,189
holding me, and she cried for the

309
00:10:15,189 --> 00:10:17,322
first time since the telegram.

310
00:10:17,502 --> 00:10:20,695
Because Walter was somewhere in France.

311
00:10:20,875 --> 00:10:22,992
And she didn't know if he would

312
00:10:22,992 --> 00:10:24,358
ever hear the bells.

313
00:10:24,708 --> 00:10:26,426
That evening Mrs.

314
00:10:26,606 --> 00:10:28,893
Pruitt came over with a pie.

315
00:10:29,073 --> 00:10:29,897
Peach.

316
00:10:30,077 --> 00:10:31,999
She was a big woman with small

317
00:10:31,999 --> 00:10:34,626
eyes who knew everything that happened on

318
00:10:34,626 --> 00:10:37,252
our street, sometimes before it happened.

319
00:10:37,432 --> 00:10:39,549
She sat down in the front room

320
00:10:39,549 --> 00:10:41,243
and held me on her knee,

321
00:10:41,243 --> 00:10:43,077
and she looked at me for a

322
00:10:43,077 --> 00:10:43,783
long time.

323
00:10:43,963 --> 00:10:44,729
Too long.

324
00:10:44,909 --> 00:10:46,407
My mother noticed.

325
00:10:46,757 --> 00:10:47,929
And then Mrs.

326
00:10:48,109 --> 00:10:50,203
Pruitt said, very sweetly,

327
00:10:50,203 --> 00:10:52,941
she's a big healthy baby for being

328
00:10:52,941 --> 00:10:54,390
so early, Dorothy.

329
00:10:54,570 --> 00:10:55,838
My mother said,

330
00:10:55,838 --> 00:10:57,275
she wasn't early.

331
00:10:57,455 --> 00:10:58,686
And Mrs.

332
00:10:58,866 --> 00:11:01,308
Pruitt smiled, and said,

333
00:11:01,308 --> 00:11:03,140
no, of course not,

334
00:11:03,140 --> 00:11:03,649
dear.

335
00:11:03,829 --> 00:11:05,957
Walter left in October,

336
00:11:05,957 --> 00:11:06,883
didn't he?

337
00:11:07,233 --> 00:11:10,361
Grandma Pearl was standing in the kitchen

338
00:11:10,361 --> 00:11:10,971
doorway.

339
00:11:11,151 --> 00:11:13,067
She told me this herself,

340
00:11:13,067 --> 00:11:14,447
forty years later,

341
00:11:14,447 --> 00:11:16,364
the week before she died.

342
00:11:16,544 --> 00:11:18,320
She said Mrs.

343
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:20,694
Pruitt handed the baby back to my

344
00:11:20,694 --> 00:11:21,159
mother.

345
00:11:21,339 --> 00:11:23,199
And then, under the table,

346
00:11:23,199 --> 00:11:25,703
where she thought nobody could see,

347
00:11:25,703 --> 00:11:27,992
she began to count the months on

348
00:11:27,992 --> 00:11:28,851
her fingers.

349
00:11:29,031 --> 00:11:30,145
October.

350
00:11:30,325 --> 00:11:31,405
November.

351
00:11:31,585 --> 00:11:32,641
December.

352
00:11:32,821 --> 00:11:33,994
January.

353
00:11:34,344 --> 00:11:35,354
Mrs.

354
00:11:35,534 --> 00:11:37,264
Pruitt stopped counting.

355
00:11:37,444 --> 00:11:39,606
She looked at my mother's face,

356
00:11:39,606 --> 00:11:41,699
and then she looked down at my

357
00:11:41,699 --> 00:11:44,839
mother's belly, still round from carrying me.

358
00:11:45,019 --> 00:11:47,179
And she didn't say another word.

359
00:11:47,359 --> 00:11:48,775
She didn't have to.

360
00:11:49,125 --> 00:11:51,110
That's enough for tonight,

361
00:11:51,110 --> 00:11:52,027
sweethearts.

362
00:11:52,207 --> 00:11:54,583
No, Jack, I'm not telling you what

363
00:11:54,583 --> 00:11:55,771
she was counting.

364
00:11:55,951 --> 00:11:57,554
You'll figure it out.

365
00:11:57,734 --> 00:11:58,848
You're smart.

366
00:11:59,028 --> 00:12:01,240
And Emma, don't you look at me

367
00:12:01,240 --> 00:12:01,977
like that.

368
00:12:02,157 --> 00:12:03,872
You'll hear it all,

369
00:12:03,872 --> 00:12:06,581
I promise, the way I heard it,

370
00:12:06,581 --> 00:12:08,206
a piece at a time.

371
00:12:08,556 --> 00:12:11,014
Tomorrow I'll tell you about the letter

372
00:12:11,014 --> 00:12:12,967
my mother hid in the flour tin.

373
00:12:13,147 --> 00:12:15,377
And the name it was signed with.

374
00:12:15,557 --> 00:12:17,611
Goodnight, sweethearts.

375
00:12:17,791 --> 00:12:19,784
And if you want to hear the

376
00:12:19,784 --> 00:12:22,440
next chapter, you know where to find

377
00:12:22,440 --> 00:12:23,399
your grandma.

378
00:12:23,579 --> 00:12:24,543
Every night.

379
00:12:24,723 --> 00:12:25,919
Right here.
